From 0cbb92f216c82c108b46846ca48bdf505d8a980d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: VAP Memory Optimization <noreply@example.com>
Date: Mon, 11 May 2026 19:59:12 +0530
Subject: [PATCH] cel: hoist the varEnvs matrix to a process-wide
 template-keyed pool
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Prerequisites: patch 0001 (process-wide compiled-program cache) and patch 0004
(lazy per-compiler varEnvs). This patch builds on 0004's compiler.envFor and
the compiler struct it reshaped; apply 0001 then 0004 then this. It is NOT
compatible with patch 0010 (the alternative to 0004).

Even with 0001/0004, policies whose `variables` signatures differ each rebuild
the per-OptionalVariableDeclarations env matrix from scratch -- cel-go env
clones whose function/library/declType machinery is byte-for-byte identical
across thousands of policies. With 0004 the matrix is per-compiler-lazy; this
patch makes it process-wide-shared.

  * compile_matrix_pool.go: a sharedVarEnvsPool keyed by env-template pointer,
    lazily materializing each (template, opts) EnvSet under sync.Once and
    caching it process-wide (matrixPoolCache).
  * compile.go: compiler grows sharedMatrix and variablesOverlay fields. envFor
    is refactored to memoize generically; the new buildEnv either pulls the
    (opts) entry from the shared pool and Extends the per-policy `variables`
    overlay on top, or -- for standalone NewCompiler -- builds from the
    compiler's own base as before.
  * composition.go: NewCompositedCompiler captures the `variables` overlay
    declaratively, points the cached compiler at sharedMatrixForTemplate(envSet),
    and wires the overlay. state.EnvSet still carries the variables-extended env
    so callers that consult it directly are unaffected.

The declaration set of the compilation env is unchanged: in both the old
(template -> variables -> opts) and the new (template -> opts -> variables)
orderings the final env is (template) ∪ (opts decls) ∪ (variables decl) ∪
(declTypes), and cel-go treats added options as additive and order-independent
for declarations of disjoint names (`variables` is disjoint from every opts
variable name).

Estimated savings: 2-6 GiB on a 10,000-policy cluster with ~100 distinct
variable signatures (the matrix retained heap collapses from O(policies) to
O(templates) -- one template in production).

Tested:
  go build  ./staging/src/k8s.io/apiserver/pkg/admission/plugin/cel/... ./.../policy/...   OK
  go vet    (same packages)                                                                OK
  go test   ./staging/src/k8s.io/apiserver/pkg/admission/plugin/cel/...                     PASS
  go test   ./staging/src/k8s.io/apiserver/pkg/admission/plugin/policy/...                  PASS
  go test   ./staging/src/k8s.io/apiserver/pkg/cel/...                                      PASS
  go test   ./staging/src/k8s.io/apiserver/pkg/admission/plugin/webhook/matchconditions/... PASS
